My journey as a technical communicator began near the start of university. Throughout highschool, I had a huge interest in computer technology.  I pursued this interest by doing science fair projects in the computer science category and begging my parents for a computer. I had a mentor through my magnet program who taught me about information science, building circuits, soldering, and programming in C/C++. My neighbor introduced me to Linux, and I developed the dream of contributing to open source software. I built little web sites to show to my friends, and even had a forum where we would sometimes chat. I selected computer science as my major when I applied for university.

However I had another strong interest. I had always easily excelled in anything regarding reading and writing. I was in the top of the AR stars at my school, a program that rewarded children who read books. My scores were exceptional on any test, grades high in those classes, and I was an enthusiastic participant in any literary conversations. I learned that there was a career called technical writing, and this seemed to me a way to participate in computer technologies I loved using my natural skills. I decided to ndulge programming as a hobby, and pursue technical communication as my career. 

I did well at and enjoyed the technical communication program at Southern Polytechnic. I learned many strategies and skills that have served me well in my career. With my electives, I would take math, computer science, electrical engineering, and physics courses. 

